# std/llm-step's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): build this directory as a
# Rust project with rustc, then bind the tools it drives.
#
# `--src:@=.` hands rustc the tool (Cargo.toml + src/);
# rustc skips the caos metadata — .caos-expr, DEPS,
# DEEP-DEPS/. `--dep0:@=…` and `--dep1:@=…` hand it the llm-client and shared
# conversation-protocol crates. `--output-runner` selects the opt-in Git
# runtime for the finished binary without changing rustc's own seeded runner.
#
# THE TOOL IMAGES ARE BOUND HERE, not by the caller. Each `curry --base:@=x`
# resolves that mount through its own `.caos-expr` to the image it builds (a
# curry with no args is the ready-to-run ArgTree), and `--k=$VAR` binds that
# object BY REFERENCE. So a caller resolves llm-step and gets a step that
# already knows its tools — it never has to name bash-tool, rgrep, merge,
# caos-build, caos-test or caos-test-result, which are llm-step's dependencies
# and not the caller's business.
GREP=curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/rgrep
MERGE=curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/merge
ASYNC_WORKER=curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/run-and-update-ref
STEP=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/rustc --src:@=. --dep0:@=DEEP-DEPS/llm-client --dep1:@=DEEP-DEPS/conversation-protocol --dep2:@=DEEP-DEPS/git-locator --output-runner:@=DEEP-DEPS/git-runner
curry --base=$STEP --grep-image=$GREP --merge-image=$MERGE --run-and-update-ref-image=$ASYNC_WORKER
